  "final": "`parse_amount` in `src/money.py` no longer goes through floats, and `make_invoice` in `src/api.py` now validates every item with the same rules. I ran my own edge-case script with the global precision set to 3, and it passed. `checks/public.py` also ran without errors, but it printed nothing, so I can't say how much it covers.\n\n- **How it stays exact:** amounts are validated and converted to whole cents as integers. The result is built directly as a `Decimal` with exponent -2. Nothing depends on the caller's decimal precision, and the code doesn't touch the global context or the input.\n- **`parse_amount`:** it accepts `Decimal` values (including exponent notation), non-bool ints, and strings that fully match the ASCII grammar. It raises `ValueError` for everything else you listed. Trailing zeros are fine, and a nonzero fractional cent is rejected rather than rounded.\n- **`make_invoice`:** it accepts only a real `list`, so strings, `None` and tuples are rejected. It sums the cent values exactly and rejects a running total over 1000000.00. It returns `{'total': '…', 'count': N}`, and an empty list gives `{'total': '0.00', 'count': 0}`.\n- **`format_amount`:** unchanged.\n- **Private helpers:** `make_invoice` imports the underscore-prefixed helpers `_to_cents`, `_from_cents` and `_MAX_CENTS` from `src/money.py`.\n\nIn my script, at precision 3 these gave the expected results:\n- `\"1234.50\"`, `Decimal(\"1E+3\")`, `Decimal(\"5.1000000\")` and `7` all parsed correctly.\n- A three-item invoice totalled `'101239.57'`.\n- `[\"600000\", \"400000\"]` totalled exactly `'1000000.00'`.\n- The inputs you said to reject all raised `ValueError`, as did `Decimal(\"1E-1000000\")`, `Decimal(\"1E+999999999\")`, `Decimal(\"NaN\")` and an invoice list totalling 1000000.01.\n- The global precision was still 3 afterwards.",
